1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,500
They save us time.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,400
They save us money.

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,740
They've changed our lives in 100 ways.

4
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:09,840
Household appliances.

5
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,040
The vacuum was invented because of a sneeze.

6
00:00:13,140 --> 00:00:16,140
The sewing machine caused riots.

7
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:20,060
And the washing machine had over 1,000 patents

8
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:22,560
before it was motorized.

9
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:28,200
Now, Yankee ingenuity in the home with household wonders

10
00:00:28,201 --> 00:00:30,200
on Modern Marvels.

11
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,200
This lady's calling her husband.

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,200
This lady's calling her mother.

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:53,200
And this lady, she's calling her oven.

14
00:00:53,201 --> 00:00:55,200
Sound funny?

15
00:00:55,201 --> 00:00:58,800
Actually, it's something that could happen in the future.

16
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:01,140
The fulfillment of the American dream,

17
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:05,480
a kitchen that runs itself, an oven that bakes by remote

18
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:06,680
control.

19
00:01:06,780 --> 00:01:10,980
That was the future in the 1950s, a wall to wall electrical

20
00:01:11,080 --> 00:01:14,920
wonder filled with the newest blender, mixer, defroster,

21
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:18,760
and disposal, all turned on with the touch of a button

22
00:01:18,860 --> 00:01:22,060
or the turn of a dial.

23
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,500
But the miracle of this household technology

24
00:01:24,501 --> 00:01:25,900
was hard earned.

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,880
Each of these inventions tells the story of the daily life

26
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:31,840
of a nation and even the world.

27
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:36,820
And their story begins with the revolution.

28
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:39,440
It was America's second revolution,

29
00:01:39,540 --> 00:01:43,260
but it was heralded not by the fire of muskets or roar

30
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:47,060
of a cannon, but by the whir of wheels and cogs

31
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:49,820
and the clatter of the factory.

32
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:54,200
It was the industrial revolution, the age of manufacturing,

33
00:01:54,201 --> 00:01:59,640
mass production, automation, and invention.

34
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:02,140
It began in the middle of the 19th century,

35
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,940
and it forever changed our lives.

36
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:08,340
A new middle class was being born.

37
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:11,260
Domestic servants, once a staple in the average home,

38
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,260
were leaving for a better job in the factories.

39
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,860
They were becoming independent, and you couldn't blame them.

40
00:02:17,960 --> 00:02:21,360
The standard work period for a living servant

41
00:02:21,361 --> 00:02:24,960
was 13 days on, one day off.

42
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:26,500
The baby cried in the middle of the night.

43
00:02:26,600 --> 00:02:30,360
You got up, not the baby's mother.

44
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:32,040
You couldn't have guests.

45
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:33,300
You had no privacy.

46
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,080
And you had no private life.

47
00:02:35,180 --> 00:02:37,540
As soon as an alternative opened up,

48
00:02:37,640 --> 00:02:40,720
servants fled the household.

49
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:42,280
Home alone.

50
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,520
Without servants, the American housewife

51
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:46,960
was now in a house without help.

52
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:50,220
And the time was right for ingenious Yankee innovators

53
00:02:50,221 --> 00:02:53,820
to create new maids to lighten the labor of the household.

54
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:55,720
It was the dawn of the new appliance.

55
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:05,460
It all started with fire, man's first stove.

56
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,200
Cave life literally revolved around it,

57
00:03:08,300 --> 00:03:12,640
and it became the heart of man's first home.

58
00:03:12,740 --> 00:03:14,920
For thousands of years, techniques of cooking

59
00:03:15,020 --> 00:03:18,280
hardly changed, and neither did our diets.

60
00:03:18,281 --> 00:03:21,080
We were slaves of the fireplace.

61
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:23,680
If you look at what travelers wrote about life

62
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:26,620
in the American colonies in the 18th century,

63
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:29,520
people ate very simple meals.

64
00:03:29,620 --> 00:03:33,000
With a fireplace, you couldn't do complicated cooking.

65
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:37,860
Horridge and stew, everything was put into one pot.

66
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:40,940
Life with an open hearth was simple and dirty,

67
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:44,600
and it took the whole family to keep their fire burning.

68
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:47,440
It was a man's work to chop the wood,

69
00:03:47,441 --> 00:03:53,200
To hole it first, then to chop it, then to get it into the house, keep the fire going.

70
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:55,400
It was children's work to clean out the ash.

71
00:03:56,640 --> 00:03:57,340
Women cooked.

72
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:02,000
The history of the cooking stove is hard to trace.

73
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:08,400
In the 1740s, Benjamin Franklin invented a heating stove designed to warm a room.

74
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:14,420
From there, we know that hundreds of independent manufacturers across the country worked on the idea.

75
00:04:14,421 --> 00:04:17,620
Some patented their designs, some did not.

76
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:20,320
But the demand for the stove actually

77
00:04:20,420 --> 00:04:23,960
coincided with developments in the iron industry.

78
00:04:24,060 --> 00:04:26,360
Early in the 1830s, it was discovered

79
00:04:26,460 --> 00:04:28,660
that pig iron made with coke or coal

80
00:04:28,760 --> 00:04:32,580
could be successfully re-melted into stove plates.

81
00:04:32,680 --> 00:04:37,480
And with that innovation, the domestic cooking stove arrived.

82
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:40,520
Much heralded and gargantuan in size,

83
00:04:40,521 --> 00:04:43,760
it was called the most important domestic symbol

84
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:45,260
of the 19th century.

85
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:56,360
Early cooking stoves were also heaters,

86
00:04:56,460 --> 00:04:59,000
and inventors came up with an astounding array

87
00:04:59,100 --> 00:05:01,180
of variations on the theme.

88
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:03,040
You could have flat ranges with lots

89
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:05,640
of different cooking holes, or a step range

90
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:09,820
with a variety of hot surfaces, or two or three ovens

91
00:05:09,821 --> 00:05:12,220
for warming and baking.

92
00:05:12,320 --> 00:05:15,560
But no matter what incarnation you happened to choose,

93
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:20,860
it was indispensable, even if your home was a covered wagon.

94
00:05:20,960 --> 00:05:23,460
The advantage of the stove was it came collapsed.

95
00:05:23,560 --> 00:05:26,580
You could put it on your wagon, haul it west,

96
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:29,280
and set it up even before you had a house,

97
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:31,380
and you could cook.

98
00:05:31,480 --> 00:05:34,220
Now, instead of one-pot meals,

99
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:37,280
your stove could make soup, boil potatoes,

100
00:05:37,281 --> 00:05:40,620
and bake a pie all at the same time.

101
00:05:40,720 --> 00:05:42,780
But as efficient as it seemed, the stove

102
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:46,820
was not greeted with wholehearted enthusiasm.

103
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:49,820
The fireplace was long a symbol of the home.

104
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:52,160
No one had ever heard of bringing up a family

105
00:05:52,260 --> 00:05:54,500
around the stove.

106
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:58,000
Some households regarded the open hearth

107
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:02,000
as the traditional American way of life,

108
00:06:02,100 --> 00:06:03,840
and they didn't want to give it up.

109
00:06:03,841 --> 00:06:06,480
It meant their connection to the past,

110
00:06:06,580 --> 00:06:08,840
their connection to their parents' homes,

111
00:06:08,940 --> 00:06:11,440
their connection to the revolution.

112
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:13,480
But in spite of the furor, stoves

113
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,780
were embraced and in vast numbers.

114
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:20,760
In the 1850s, laborers earned about $1 a day.

115
00:06:20,860 --> 00:06:26,260
Stoves cost anywhere from $5 to $25.

116
00:06:26,360 --> 00:06:28,840
But popular as they became, over time,

117
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:31,960
women were discovering a secret.

118
00:06:31,961 --> 00:06:34,000
If anything, this modern invention

119
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:36,700
kept her in the kitchen even longer.

120
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:41,340
She was cooking more and more complicated meals.

121
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,740
It was literally changing her life,

122
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,480
as it would soon change her husband's when coal

123
00:06:46,580 --> 00:06:48,980
became the stove's new fuel.

124
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:52,080
You couldn't go out to your backyard and get coal.

125
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:55,480
You had to buy it, which meant the household had to have cash,

126
00:06:55,580 --> 00:06:59,480
which meant that somebody had to be working in what we call

127
00:06:59,580 --> 00:07:00,920
the marketplace.

128
00:07:00,921 --> 00:07:03,660
And that, generally speaking, would be Dan.

129
00:07:03,760 --> 00:07:05,960
With the invention of gas and electricity

130
00:07:06,060 --> 00:07:10,660
in the late 19th century came cooler, faster cooking for women

131
00:07:10,760 --> 00:07:13,960
and the elimination of heavy work for men.

132
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:20,180
But more than that, it also changed our habits in little

133
00:07:20,280 --> 00:07:21,180
ways.

134
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:24,180
The possibility of just turning on one burner in the summer

135
00:07:24,280 --> 00:07:26,680
was absolutely celebrated.

136
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:29,040
No one who hasn't cooked over a wood stove

137
00:07:29,041 --> 00:07:32,780
stove by the light of a kerosene lamp can really appreciate

138
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:34,280
what it all means.

139
00:07:34,380 --> 00:07:36,340
The steps, it would say.

140
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:38,280
Good old days, eh, Grandma?

141
00:07:38,380 --> 00:07:43,180
Yes, and anybody who wants them can have my share.

142
00:07:43,280 --> 00:07:44,860
But the evolution of the stove

143
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:48,260
didn't stop with the miracle of electricity.

144
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:50,120
It was to make one more mighty leap

145
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:55,060
with the sweet discovery of the microwave.

146
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:58,360
The original microwave ovens of the 1950s

147
00:07:58,361 --> 00:08:04,240
were big, cumbersome, expensive, and not too efficient.

148
00:08:04,340 --> 00:08:06,540
In fact, they looked like they came straight out

149
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:10,240
of a science lab, which is exactly where they did come

150
00:08:10,340 --> 00:08:11,040
from.

151
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:16,980
It was 1945 and the end of World War II,

152
00:08:17,080 --> 00:08:19,180
and Percy Spencer was making his rounds

153
00:08:19,280 --> 00:08:21,980
at the Raytheon radar labs.

154
00:08:22,080 --> 00:08:24,460
He was working around the magnetron, a machine that

155
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:26,160
produces radio waves.

156
00:08:26,161 --> 00:08:30,160
when he discovered a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted.

157
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:35,100
The funny thing was, he had felt no heat.

158
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,900
The next day, he put Indian corn in front of the equipment

159
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:39,400
and watched the kernels pop.

160
00:08:43,080 --> 00:08:45,140
Spencer's early experiments revealed

161
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:48,420
that when high frequency radio waves were housed in a metal

162
00:08:48,520 --> 00:08:51,380
box, you could cook food quickly.

163
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:53,960
He learned that radio waves penetrate the food

164
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:56,900
to cause certain molecules to vibrate.

165
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,960
The vibrations generate heat, and thus the food is cooked.

166
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,400
And so the microwave oven, or radar range,

167
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,900
as it was first called, was born.

168
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:09,800
But it was not accepted.

169
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:12,420
Were they going to use it to boil water?

170
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,520
Well, what were its advantages for boiling water?

171
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:18,320
It was not, in fact, faster for boiling water.

172
00:09:18,420 --> 00:09:22,120
Were they going to use it for fancy cooking?

173
00:09:22,121 --> 00:09:25,760
People who had microwaves had to figure out what to do with it.

174
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:28,420
It was the food industry that finally figured it out

175
00:09:28,520 --> 00:09:30,800
when they started supplying microwave products

176
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:31,800
to be reheated.

177
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:36,340
And it was then the radar range became the king of reheating.

178
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:39,240
But the burden of cooking was not the only domestic chore

179
00:09:39,340 --> 00:09:41,240
whose load needed to be lightened.

180
00:09:41,340 --> 00:09:43,240
The housewife of the 19th century

181
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:46,740
was still washing her clothes in a wooden tub

182
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:49,480
and cleaning her carpets with a common broom.

183
00:09:49,481 --> 00:09:52,720
But all that was about to change.

184
00:09:52,820 --> 00:09:55,620
In 1908 in Germany, Mrs. Melita Bence

185
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:57,980
wanted to make a better cup of coffee.

186
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:01,020
She punched holes in a tin can and cut a round disk

187
00:10:01,120 --> 00:10:04,320
from blotter paper, inventing the first Melita filter.

188
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:13,940
Advertising executives of the 1950s

189
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:16,980
envisioned the American housewife as Eve,

190
00:10:16,981 --> 00:10:20,420
living fruitfully in her electrical garden of Eden.

191
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:23,280
A bit theatrical and far-fetched, perhaps,

192
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:26,460
but electricity had long been the siren song for selling

193
00:10:26,560 --> 00:10:28,220
the household appliance.

194
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:32,560
Mother, wouldn't it be slick if we had an electric ironer

195
00:10:32,660 --> 00:10:34,500
like Sally's mother has?

196
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:35,700
Of course it would.

197
00:10:35,800 --> 00:10:39,200
And I'm looking forward to having an electric dryer, too.

198
00:10:39,300 --> 00:10:40,400
Just think.

199
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:42,600
Then we'd have a complete electric laundry.

200
00:10:42,700 --> 00:10:43,980
Whee!

201
00:10:43,981 --> 00:10:47,520
It was to be the modern way, and it all started

202
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:52,760
with the illumination of just one incandescent bulb.

203
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:56,320
Electricity, Thomas Alva Edison's brilliant invention

204
00:10:56,420 --> 00:10:59,700
in 1879, not only switched on light,

205
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:03,560
it inaugurated a new spirit and fervor of creativity

206
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:05,100
and invention.

207
00:11:05,200 --> 00:11:06,500
It was a new age for man.

208
00:11:10,240 --> 00:11:13,980
In 1917, General Electric called electricity the law

209
00:11:13,981 --> 00:11:17,220
that lights the way to lighter housework.

210
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:20,420
And well it would be, but not so soon.

211
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:23,420
The electric home was still a vision of the future.

212
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:25,220
Even if the average turn-of-the-century

213
00:11:25,320 --> 00:11:27,220
Victorian homemaker was lucky enough

214
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:30,160
to own an electric appliance, she probably

215
00:11:30,260 --> 00:11:33,260
had nothing to plug it into.

216
00:11:33,360 --> 00:11:36,240
The first thing was to get electricity into the home.

217
00:11:36,340 --> 00:11:38,540
But for the most part, what came into the home

218
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:40,240
was a light bulb.

219
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:42,680
People didn't have outlets, they didn't have appliances,

220
00:11:42,681 --> 00:11:45,020
they didn't have anything other than a light bulb,

221
00:11:45,120 --> 00:11:47,580
although there were a few appliances that were sold with

222
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:50,920
ways that you could screw it into the light bulb.

223
00:11:51,020 --> 00:11:54,820
Only for the wealthy, only for the very rich.

224
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:57,820
Early appliances were luxury items.

225
00:11:57,920 --> 00:12:01,740
Toasters and waffle irons never developed for the mass market.

226
00:12:01,840 --> 00:12:06,440
They were fancy and expensive and for the dining table.

227
00:12:06,540 --> 00:12:10,040
With a cord plugged above into the sconce or chandelier,

228
00:12:10,041 --> 00:12:12,940
They were not for those without time on their hands.

229
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:17,740
It was a dilemma.

230
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:21,280
To be middle class in 1919 meant to have servants.

231
00:12:21,380 --> 00:12:23,320
But there was an acute servant shortage

232
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:25,420
since the Industrial Revolution.

233
00:12:25,520 --> 00:12:28,860
So new advertisements declared, don't go to the employment

234
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:31,160
bureau, go to your lighting company.

235
00:12:31,260 --> 00:12:34,160
Let electricity be your new maid.

236
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:36,240
Just like your husband would go off

237
00:12:36,340 --> 00:12:39,240
to his new white collar job in a corporation

238
00:12:39,241 --> 00:12:40,480
and be a manager.

239
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:42,640
So you would be the manager of your household.

240
00:12:42,740 --> 00:12:43,740
And what would you manage?

241
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:45,740
Well, you would manage your appliances who

242
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:47,680
were doing the work for you.

243
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:50,080
It was a field day for inventors.

244
00:12:50,180 --> 00:12:53,180
And there was an overwhelming array of new and wondrous

245
00:12:53,280 --> 00:12:56,900
electrical servants to choose from.

246
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,160
But by far the easiest selling electrical household utility

247
00:13:00,260 --> 00:13:04,600
ever placed on the market was said to be the sewing machine.

248
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:08,240
It was the machine that freed women from slavery to the needle.

249
00:13:08,241 --> 00:13:12,240
Before that, sewing by hand was part of the fabric of daily life,

250
00:13:12,340 --> 00:13:14,980
even for wealthy women.

251
00:13:15,080 --> 00:13:17,980
The word work, if there was one thing it meant,

252
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:19,480
it meant sewing.

253
00:13:19,580 --> 00:13:22,280
They would say, she took her work with her,

254
00:13:22,380 --> 00:13:25,000
meaning she took her sewing with her.

255
00:13:25,100 --> 00:13:27,200
They worked together would probably

256
00:13:27,300 --> 00:13:29,200
mean they sewed together.

257
00:13:29,300 --> 00:13:31,100
People had much smaller wardrobes.

258
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:33,500
Even wealthy people had much smaller wardrobes.

259
00:13:33,501 --> 00:13:39,900
But it's staggering to think about the seams, literally,

260
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:45,540
that had to be sewed to keep a family, a person, a nation

261
00:13:45,640 --> 00:13:46,340
clothed.

262
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:56,760
There is no sole inventor of the sewing machine.

263
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:59,700
It's one device that seems to have occurred at the same time

264
00:13:59,800 --> 00:14:02,700
to tailors and inventors all over the world.

265
00:14:02,701 --> 00:14:06,100
But it is France who claims the first inventor who deserves

266
00:14:06,200 --> 00:14:09,740
a place of honor, Bartholomew Thamonniere,

267
00:14:09,840 --> 00:14:11,640
a man of great perseverance.

268
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:19,280
Thamonniere was a poor tailor who patented a machine in 1830

269
00:14:19,380 --> 00:14:23,460
which made a chain stitch like an embroiderer might use.

270
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:28,460
When he perfected it in 1841, he sewed 200 stitches per minute.

271
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:30,460
Realizing the potential for tailoring,

272
00:14:30,461 --> 00:14:32,560
he built a plant with eight machines,

273
00:14:32,660 --> 00:14:36,400
turning out uniforms for the French army.

274
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:39,700
But threatened Paris Taylor stormed the plant at night

275
00:14:39,800 --> 00:14:40,600
and burned it down.

276
00:14:43,300 --> 00:14:46,780
The Monniere started over again, but his second factory

277
00:14:46,880 --> 00:14:49,340
was burned by another angry mob.

278
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:52,540
His spirit broken, he died in bitter poverty.

279
00:14:57,020 --> 00:14:58,620
But the man who is considered today

280
00:14:58,621 --> 00:15:00,660
to be the father of the sewing machine

281
00:15:00,760 --> 00:15:02,720
was not the first American inventor

282
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:05,120
to produce a working model.

283
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:10,040
But he is the man whose name we remember, Elias Howe, Jr.

284
00:15:10,140 --> 00:15:15,000
He was a lame and sickly mechanic at a Boston cotton mill.

285
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:17,940
He was often bedridden and watched his young wife sewing

286
00:15:18,040 --> 00:15:21,040
at home to keep the household afloat.

287
00:15:21,140 --> 00:15:22,840
His first efforts at a sewing machine

288
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:25,180
mimicked his wife's hand motions.

289
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:27,340
But later, struck with sudden insight,

290
00:15:27,341 --> 00:15:30,680
he devised a lock stitch using two threads and a shuttle.

291
00:15:33,080 --> 00:15:37,380
But at $300, his invention was too expensive for home use.

292
00:15:37,480 --> 00:15:41,980
And Boston clothing factory owners rejected it.

293
00:15:42,080 --> 00:15:44,460
Discouraged, he set sail for England,

294
00:15:44,560 --> 00:15:46,500
only to be swindled and mistreated

295
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:49,000
by a ruthless corset maker.

296
00:15:49,100 --> 00:15:51,540
When he returned to America, he was penniless.

297
00:15:51,640 --> 00:15:54,340
His wife was dying, and he found that Boston firms

298
00:15:54,440 --> 00:15:57,100
were selling sewing machines much like his own design

299
00:15:57,101 --> 00:15:59,600
to factories.

300
00:15:59,700 --> 00:16:03,040
Howe sued the manufacturers and fought for his own patent

301
00:16:03,140 --> 00:16:04,900
and won.

302
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:08,040
One notable infringer was ex-actor and inventor

303
00:16:08,140 --> 00:16:10,940
promoter Isaac Singer.

304
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:13,440
Singer was a handsome figure.

305
00:16:13,540 --> 00:16:15,860
Once a Shakespearean actor on the stage,

306
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:18,460
he was also a resourceful inventor.

307
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:20,660
If we are to believe Singer himself,

308
00:16:20,760 --> 00:16:23,260
he created his patent model of a sewing machine

309
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:27,080
in only 11 days, introducing the tradel

310
00:16:27,100 --> 00:16:30,100
a presser foot that kept the cloth firmly down,

311
00:16:30,200 --> 00:16:33,440
and a cabinet to store it in.

312
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:35,240
But there was one problem.

313
00:16:35,340 --> 00:16:37,640
It was expensive.

314
00:16:37,740 --> 00:16:39,740
But that didn't stop Isaac Singer.

315
00:16:39,840 --> 00:16:41,840
As clever as he was as an inventor,

316
00:16:41,940 --> 00:16:45,760
Singer was at heart a promoter, one of the best of the times.

317
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:50,660
Around 1857, his company was selling 1,000 machines a week

318
00:16:50,760 --> 00:16:53,860
and making $1,000 a day.

319
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:57,100
He had found a way to make the sewing machine affordable.

320
00:16:57,101 --> 00:17:00,000
He and a partner invented the installment plan.

321
00:17:00,001 --> 00:17:02,300
The fact that you could get a Singer sewing machine

322
00:17:02,301 --> 00:17:05,200
and take it home for $5, even though you might eventually

323
00:17:05,201 --> 00:17:09,140
spend $120 to own it, was just tremendously attractive.

324
00:17:09,141 --> 00:17:11,840
He really was one of the last major manufacturers

325
00:17:11,841 --> 00:17:13,960
to adapt to mass production.

326
00:17:13,961 --> 00:17:17,660
His machines were virtually handmade and hand fitted

327
00:17:17,661 --> 00:17:20,760
well into the early 1880s.

328
00:17:20,761 --> 00:17:23,660
But he could afford to do this because he had such a fantastic

329
00:17:23,661 --> 00:17:24,960
marketing plan.

330
00:17:24,961 --> 00:17:28,960
Until 1912, the American woman's foot was still chained

331
00:17:29,060 --> 00:17:30,960
to the sewing machine's treadle.

332
00:17:31,060 --> 00:17:32,900
But in that year, Hamilton Beach

333
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:36,600
invented the home motor that would not only run the machine,

334
00:17:36,700 --> 00:17:40,000
it would sharpen knives, polish silver, and operate

335
00:17:40,100 --> 00:17:42,420
a cake batter mixer.

336
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:45,680
More and more electricity was freeing us from our slavery

337
00:17:45,780 --> 00:17:46,720
to the home.

338
00:17:46,820 --> 00:17:51,620
But for all it could give, it could also take away.

339
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:54,720
Beginning with the first years of the 19th century,

340
00:17:54,721 --> 00:17:58,800
America's huge commercial ice business boomed.

341
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:00,720
But the invention of the refrigerator

342
00:18:00,820 --> 00:18:04,860
threatened to melt the fortunes of the men who made big money

343
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:08,140
from those big blocks of ice.

344
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:10,240
As the patent holder for the sewing machine,

345
00:18:10,340 --> 00:18:13,800
Elias Howe was paid $25 for each sewing machine

346
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:15,400
manufactured.

347
00:18:15,500 --> 00:18:21,340
He earned over $2 million before he died in 1867.

348
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:27,160
Bottoms up there, sonny boy.

349
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:29,160
Mm, is that good to the last gurgle?

350
00:18:29,260 --> 00:18:30,360
And why not?

351
00:18:30,460 --> 00:18:33,600
The milk has been kept just right in mother's GE monitor top.

352
00:18:33,700 --> 00:18:36,600
The refrigerator, another modern convenience

353
00:18:36,700 --> 00:18:39,340
we take for granted even as we sneak that second dish

354
00:18:39,440 --> 00:18:40,840
of ice cream.

355
00:18:40,940 --> 00:18:43,780
But to remember what life was like without it just a few

356
00:18:43,880 --> 00:18:48,780
generations ago, it meant smoking meats, pickling vegetables,

357
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:51,520
and daily shopping, or you did without.

358
00:18:51,521 --> 00:18:54,320
you did without.

359
00:18:54,420 --> 00:18:57,560
Originally, ice was a luxury.

360
00:18:57,660 --> 00:19:01,860
But by 1799, it became a necessity for food preservation

361
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:03,500
and sanitation.

362
00:19:03,600 --> 00:19:06,860
And that meant the harvesting of ice.

363
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:09,640
Prior to the 1800s, ice was primarily

364
00:19:09,740 --> 00:19:12,880
harvested by farmers for the storage of meats and dairy

365
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:14,380
products.

366
00:19:14,480 --> 00:19:17,820
But by the early 19th century, commercial ice became

367
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:20,520
an enterprising business.

368
00:19:20,521 --> 00:19:24,760
All over the north, every pond, every lake, in some cases,

369
00:19:24,860 --> 00:19:28,260
rivers, companies took the ice.

370
00:19:28,360 --> 00:19:30,660
There's a lovely, lovely description of it

371
00:19:30,760 --> 00:19:33,760
in Thoreau's Walden, where he talks about,

372
00:19:33,860 --> 00:19:36,440
at some point during the winter while he's there,

373
00:19:36,540 --> 00:19:38,840
this ice company comes to take, I think he says,

374
00:19:38,940 --> 00:19:41,780
the very skin off Walden Pond.

375
00:19:41,880 --> 00:19:43,540
Merchants from the north cut ice

376
00:19:43,640 --> 00:19:47,440
and shipped it to the south, or even to the West Indies.

377
00:19:47,441 --> 00:19:50,540
By the late 1800s, Maine had become the leading ice

378
00:19:50,640 --> 00:19:51,940
harvesting state.

379
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:55,240
And during the winter season, work was frantic.

380
00:19:55,340 --> 00:19:59,460
On the frozen Kennebec River, it took an army of 4,000 men

381
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:05,300
and 350 horses working night and day to cut the ice.

382
00:20:05,400 --> 00:20:08,100
But ice harvesting was expensive.

383
00:20:08,200 --> 00:20:11,800
All those men and horses, and much would be lost in storing

384
00:20:11,900 --> 00:20:13,740
and shipping.

385
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:15,700
It would just melt away.

386
00:20:15,701 --> 00:20:20,040
It was really for commercial use only.

387
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:23,180
But as cities began to expand and people moved further

388
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:26,340
from where food was grown, the demand for mechanical

389
00:20:26,440 --> 00:20:29,640
refrigeration grew.

390
00:20:29,740 --> 00:20:32,820
Between 1830 and 1880, dozens and dozens

391
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:34,980
of mechanical refrigerating machines

392
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:38,960
were patented, using the idea of air circulation

393
00:20:39,060 --> 00:20:41,160
for efficient cooling.

394
00:20:41,260 --> 00:20:45,200
Finally, by the end of 1880, they were perfected.

395
00:20:45,201 --> 00:20:48,140
Powered by steam engines, the first refrigerators

396
00:20:48,240 --> 00:20:54,180
were for commercial purposes, and they were huge and hazardous.

397
00:20:54,280 --> 00:20:56,440
It was those systems that finally brought ice

398
00:20:56,540 --> 00:20:58,320
to our doorsteps.

399
00:20:58,420 --> 00:21:02,580
It was to be a new way of life with ice and the Iceman.

400
00:21:10,920 --> 00:21:14,260
As early as 1830, ice boxes appeared

401
00:21:14,261 --> 00:21:16,060
in American homes.

402
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:21,160
Made mainly of oak, many doubled as a major piece of furniture.

403
00:21:21,260 --> 00:21:23,600
In the summer, ice was delivered right to your house

404
00:21:23,700 --> 00:21:27,200
six days a week and twice on Saturday.

405
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:30,340
Early each day, a cardboard sign was placed in your window

406
00:21:30,440 --> 00:21:32,180
to show how much ice you needed.

407
00:21:32,280 --> 00:21:34,620
And then the Iceman arrived.

408
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:36,120
Well, the Iceman tramping through your house,

409
00:21:36,220 --> 00:21:37,820
it wasn't just that he was a strange man

410
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:39,180
tramping through the house.

411
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:43,760
The Iceman dripped as he tramped through the house.

412
00:21:43,761 --> 00:21:51,080
And again, people who could afford it or people who were particularly ingenious had various ways of dealing with this.

413
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:57,280
Some people had holes cut in their sides of their houses for the ice delivery.

414
00:21:57,580 --> 00:21:59,220
Put it down there and get out.

415
00:22:00,100 --> 00:22:06,100
By the late 19th century and early 20th, our nation was devouring ice at an astonishing rate.

416
00:22:06,101 --> 00:22:09,700
In 1880, it's estimated that New York City and its close

417
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:15,140
communities alone consumed 1,500,000 tons of ice per year.

418
00:22:22,860 --> 00:22:26,660
The first domestic refrigerators to go into large-scale

419
00:22:26,760 --> 00:22:30,220
production were compression machines using sulfur dioxide

420
00:22:30,320 --> 00:22:33,860
as a refrigerant, and they were expensive.

421
00:22:33,861 --> 00:22:37,700
By 1923, the cheapest model was $450,

422
00:22:37,800 --> 00:22:41,100
when most people earned less than $2,000 a year.

423
00:22:41,200 --> 00:22:42,900
And there was another problem.

424
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,800
Once every three months, they had to be serviced.

425
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:49,500
Ironically, people really needed to be sold on the idea

426
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:51,520
of a mechanical refrigerator.

427
00:22:51,620 --> 00:22:54,520
One of the selling points of mechanical refrigerators

428
00:22:54,620 --> 00:22:59,020
in the 20s and 30s was that you could prepare certain kinds

429
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:03,860
of cold dishes like jell-os, previously known as egg.

430
00:23:03,861 --> 00:23:06,860
aspects with this new kind of control

431
00:23:06,861 --> 00:23:09,140
that you could have in the refrigerator.

432
00:23:09,141 --> 00:23:11,940
And I think it was Frigidaire that published a book called

433
00:23:11,941 --> 00:23:13,040
Cooking with Cold.

434
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:20,300
By 1923, it was clear that every household in the United

435
00:23:20,301 --> 00:23:22,740
States was going to be equipped with either gas

436
00:23:22,741 --> 00:23:24,640
or electric service.

437
00:23:24,641 --> 00:23:27,140
So if the price could be brought low enough,

438
00:23:27,141 --> 00:23:29,920
every household would be a potential customer

439
00:23:29,921 --> 00:23:32,120
for the refrigerators.

440
00:23:32,121 --> 00:23:34,480
The race was on.

441
00:23:34,580 --> 00:23:38,060
Great minds and big money were employed to develop ideas,

442
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:41,900
and it was General Electric and Monitor Top that won.

443
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,900
This modern kitchen boasts a General Electric Monitor Top

444
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:48,100
refrigerator, universally accepted everywhere

445
00:23:48,200 --> 00:23:50,540
as the standard of excellence.

446
00:23:50,640 --> 00:23:53,600
Introduced in 1925, the Monitor Top

447
00:23:53,700 --> 00:23:56,740
was a switch from water to air cooling.

448
00:23:56,840 --> 00:24:00,580
A breakthrough idea, it housed the evaporator coil and motor

449
00:24:00,581 --> 00:24:04,120
on top to blow air throughout the box.

450
00:24:04,220 --> 00:24:06,780
And it was a hit, but not necessarily

451
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:09,380
because it was a better machine.

452
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:13,320
The gas-operated refrigerator was a big competitor.

453
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:17,200
But GE began mass-producing the monitor top first.

454
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:22,300
They spent $18 million on the construction of an assembly line.

455
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:27,480
They were to win the refrigerator wars with a new low price.

456
00:24:27,580 --> 00:24:29,380
Nothing was wrong with the gas refrigerators.

457
00:24:29,381 --> 00:24:32,240
They were actually wonderful in many ways.

458
00:24:32,241 --> 00:24:33,320
They made no noise.

459
00:24:33,321 --> 00:24:39,320
The early electric refrigerators were awful.

460
00:24:39,321 --> 00:24:43,820
They just made such a racket, the motors made such a racket, that many people put them

461
00:24:43,821 --> 00:24:49,260
on their back porches rather than have them in their kitchens.

462
00:24:49,261 --> 00:24:59,380
Amazingly, in 1929, the year we entered the depression, 50,000 monitor tops were

463
00:24:59,381 --> 00:25:01,380
mass production brought the price down,

464
00:25:01,480 --> 00:25:03,980
and payment plans made purchase possible.

465
00:25:07,920 --> 00:25:10,220
And by the time we entered World War II,

466
00:25:10,320 --> 00:25:15,620
45% of American homes had mechanical refrigerators.

467
00:25:15,720 --> 00:25:18,660
Rationing and doing without didn't stop the invasion

468
00:25:18,760 --> 00:25:20,100
of the icebox.

469
00:25:20,200 --> 00:25:22,340
In the first six months after the war,

470
00:25:22,440 --> 00:25:24,900
there will be a ready market for billions of dollars

471
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:28,200
worth of things that priorities have made unobtainable.

472
00:25:29,380 --> 00:25:35,380
But after the war, manufacturers faced still another problem.

473
00:25:35,480 --> 00:25:38,120
By the 1950s, they were reaching saturation.

474
00:25:38,220 --> 00:25:42,320
About 95% of American family households had refrigerators.

475
00:25:42,420 --> 00:25:44,720
So how do you get people to buy more?

476
00:25:44,820 --> 00:25:47,300
Well, like everyone else in the 50s,

477
00:25:47,400 --> 00:25:51,300
they decided to think cars.

478
00:25:51,400 --> 00:25:54,980
The Oldsmobile Holiday, the Buick Skylark,

479
00:25:55,080 --> 00:25:56,880
and the Catalyst.

480
00:25:56,881 --> 00:26:02,480
Buick Skylark and the Cadillac 60 Special.

481
00:26:02,580 --> 00:26:04,480
So they looked to the automobile industry

482
00:26:04,580 --> 00:26:07,020
and thought, well, the automobile industry

483
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:11,420
has succeeded in making cars into fashion statements,

484
00:26:11,520 --> 00:26:12,720
into style.

485
00:26:12,820 --> 00:26:15,040
And so why don't we try that?

486
00:26:15,140 --> 00:26:17,340
For example, Frigidaire, which was owned by General Motors,

487
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:21,640
they actually had their new refrigerators previewed,

488
00:26:21,740 --> 00:26:25,280
just like their new cars at the annual Motorama fashion shows.

489
00:26:25,281 --> 00:26:27,320
style every year.

490
00:26:27,420 --> 00:26:30,860
For a period in the 1950s, refrigerator manufacturers

491
00:26:30,960 --> 00:26:33,760
made facelifts or cosmetic changes on their models

492
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:38,720
every 12 months, just like Detroit was doing with cars.

493
00:26:38,820 --> 00:26:43,200
They even borrowed the new cars' palettes of color.

494
00:26:43,300 --> 00:26:49,040
They made ice boxes in turquoise, pink, and yellow,

495
00:26:49,140 --> 00:26:52,240
soon to be classic colors of the 50s.

496
00:26:52,241 --> 00:26:54,880
But when they copied automakers' two tones,

497
00:26:54,980 --> 00:26:58,080
consumers knew they had gone too far.

498
00:26:58,180 --> 00:27:02,780
Women didn't like the idea of having all these different colors

499
00:27:02,880 --> 00:27:05,480
in their kitchen because it created a decorating problem.

500
00:27:05,580 --> 00:27:06,780
And if they wanted to redecorate,

501
00:27:06,880 --> 00:27:10,300
they're stuck with this multicolored pastel

502
00:27:10,400 --> 00:27:12,000
refrigerator.

503
00:27:12,100 --> 00:27:14,900
But there was more in store for refrigerated air

504
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:18,400
than being put in a box for preserving food.

505
00:27:18,500 --> 00:27:21,900
In 1914, air conditioning was put to use solely

506
00:27:21,901 --> 00:27:23,660
for human comfort.

507
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:28,080
Today, about 15% of all electricity generated in the

508
00:27:28,180 --> 00:27:30,680
United States goes to space cooling.

509
00:27:30,780 --> 00:27:33,440
And on especially hot days, a third or more

510
00:27:33,540 --> 00:27:35,980
goes to cooling us off.

511
00:27:36,080 --> 00:27:38,580
The long hot summer has come to an end.

512
00:27:41,180 --> 00:27:44,820
In the summer of 1881, the popular joke of the day was,

513
00:27:44,920 --> 00:27:46,360
boy, is it hot.

514
00:27:46,460 --> 00:27:47,400
How hot is it?

515
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:49,900
It's so hot that the president has a standing order

516
00:27:49,901 --> 00:27:52,840
order for five tons of ice a day.

517
00:27:52,940 --> 00:27:55,400
The president was James A. Garfield,

518
00:27:55,500 --> 00:27:59,840
and he was shot on July 2nd, three months after taking

519
00:27:59,940 --> 00:28:01,240
office.

520
00:28:01,340 --> 00:28:04,740
Doctors were sure he would never recover in the heat and humidity

521
00:28:04,840 --> 00:28:06,380
of a Washington summer.

522
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:10,120
So engineers were hired to rig a device to suck hot air

523
00:28:10,220 --> 00:28:13,900
from the outdoors and blow it over ice and into a duct

524
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,800
leading to the sick room of the president.

525
00:28:16,801 --> 00:28:20,740
And so the first air conditioner was born.

526
00:28:20,840 --> 00:28:24,040
It lowered the temperature in his room 20 degrees

527
00:28:24,140 --> 00:28:29,740
and used more than 500,000 pounds of ice.

528
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:32,520
22 years later, Willis Haviland Carrier

529
00:28:32,620 --> 00:28:35,480
invented mechanical air conditioning.

530
00:28:35,580 --> 00:28:38,380
A young engineer just eight months out of Cornell

531
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:41,900
University, he designed a machine for a printing company

532
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:46,160
that blew indoor air over refrigerated pipes.

533
00:28:46,161 --> 00:28:49,300
The invention was called the apparatus for treating air.

534
00:28:52,500 --> 00:28:54,500
Perhaps the first time most Americans

535
00:28:54,600 --> 00:28:57,300
felt air conditioning was on a Saturday afternoon

536
00:28:57,400 --> 00:28:58,800
at the movies.

537
00:28:58,900 --> 00:29:02,240
And when the Wrigley Theater opened in 1924 in New York,

538
00:29:02,340 --> 00:29:05,520
with Carrier's new centrifugal refrigeration equipment,

539
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:07,420
it became the talk of Broadway.

540
00:29:11,520 --> 00:29:15,420
A truly historic event occurred near Christmas 1928.

541
00:29:15,421 --> 00:29:17,720
The carrier system was officially inaugurated

542
00:29:17,820 --> 00:29:21,320
on the opening session of the US House of Representatives.

543
00:29:21,420 --> 00:29:23,520
The next year, the Senate followed.

544
00:29:23,620 --> 00:29:26,940
A doctor had testified that the foul air in the Capitol

545
00:29:27,040 --> 00:29:30,900
impaired the mental faculties of members of Congress.

546
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:32,980
Air conditioned the place, he said,

547
00:29:33,080 --> 00:29:36,740
and the quality of legislation will improve.

548
00:29:36,840 --> 00:29:40,480
For the record, the air in Congress was cooler,

549
00:29:40,580 --> 00:29:42,620
but mental faculties stayed the same.

550
00:29:45,420 --> 00:29:48,420
In the late 1930s, the first room units appeared.

551
00:29:48,520 --> 00:29:51,200
They were made possible by the invention of Freon,

552
00:29:51,300 --> 00:29:53,560
a non-flammable refrigerant.

553
00:29:53,660 --> 00:29:56,860
With it, cooling units could be made smaller, safer,

554
00:29:56,960 --> 00:29:58,900
and less expensive.

555
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,340
And by the 1950s, the window cooler

556
00:30:01,440 --> 00:30:03,880
began conquering the country.

557
00:30:03,980 --> 00:30:06,880
General Electric had proved a more formidable invader

558
00:30:06,980 --> 00:30:08,280
than General Sherman.

559
00:30:08,380 --> 00:30:12,380
By 1980, 73% of homes in the southern states

560
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:15,420
were air conditioned.

561
00:30:15,421 --> 00:30:18,460
The invention of the refrigerator and refrigerated air

562
00:30:18,560 --> 00:30:21,200
was a major technological breakthrough,

563
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:25,200
and for all practical purposes, inspired by necessity.

564
00:30:25,300 --> 00:30:27,600
Not so the washing machine.

565
00:30:27,700 --> 00:30:30,240
Its inspiration came from liberation,

566
00:30:30,340 --> 00:30:34,540
setting women free from the endless blue Mondays of laundry.

567
00:30:37,100 --> 00:30:41,180
Kitchen appliances got a public relations boost in 1935

568
00:30:41,280 --> 00:30:44,380
with the establishment of the National Kitchen Modernization

569
00:30:44,381 --> 00:30:46,380
Modernization Bureau.

570
00:30:49,380 --> 00:30:54,380
Boy, here's real emancipation from old-fashioned chores.

571
00:30:54,381 --> 00:30:57,380
Just set a dial and walk away.

572
00:30:57,381 --> 00:31:02,380
Laundry was very horrible, and I think the best indication of how horrible it was was

573
00:31:02,381 --> 00:31:07,380
that it was always the first task that any woman with any amount of money would get rid of,

574
00:31:07,381 --> 00:31:11,380
would find someone to help her do.

575
00:31:11,381 --> 00:31:14,120
More than any other appliance, the washing machine

576
00:31:14,220 --> 00:31:16,180
set women free.

577
00:31:16,280 --> 00:31:20,620
Monday was wash day, and it took all day.

578
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:23,860
It was Monday because people changed their clothes on Sunday

579
00:31:23,960 --> 00:31:26,220
and Tuesday was for ironing.

580
00:31:26,320 --> 00:31:30,360
That's the way the week always went when laundry was still

581
00:31:30,460 --> 00:31:31,340
a dirty word.

582
00:31:33,940 --> 00:31:35,040
It was hard.

583
00:31:35,140 --> 00:31:36,740
It was hot.

584
00:31:36,840 --> 00:31:38,740
It was exhausting.

585
00:31:38,741 --> 00:31:43,220
Even in 1833, with the invention of the washboard.

586
00:31:44,020 --> 00:31:50,020
It was an enormous amount of backbreaking hauling of work.

587
00:31:50,380 --> 00:31:55,660
The worst part, however, was wringing the clothing dry.

588
00:31:56,100 --> 00:31:59,120
These fabrics dry were heavy.

589
00:32:00,020 --> 00:32:01,680
Wet, they weighed a ton.

590
00:32:02,280 --> 00:32:06,920
During the 1800s, the patent office was overflowing with new inventions

591
00:32:06,921 --> 00:32:09,420
for a mechanical washing machine.

592
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:11,520
Some with agitators were even invented

593
00:32:11,620 --> 00:32:15,060
as a combination washing machine, butter churn.

594
00:32:15,160 --> 00:32:17,160
Now, they weren't mechanical in the way

595
00:32:17,260 --> 00:32:18,460
that we think of mechanical.

596
00:32:18,560 --> 00:32:21,860
They didn't run on electric energy or even a gasoline

597
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:23,160
motor.

598
00:32:23,260 --> 00:32:27,880
They were powered by human energy.

599
00:32:27,980 --> 00:32:30,420
Some of them were based on a crank principle,

600
00:32:30,520 --> 00:32:34,080
and you had to turn the crank.

601
00:32:34,180 --> 00:32:36,580
And that was hard work.

602
00:32:36,581 --> 00:32:41,680
It was the most common invention of the 19th century.

603
00:32:41,780 --> 00:32:47,920
Between 1804 and 1873, 1,676 patents for washers

604
00:32:48,020 --> 00:32:52,520
were issued by the US Patent Office.

605
00:32:52,620 --> 00:32:54,500
Why so many?

606
00:32:54,600 --> 00:32:57,360
You didn't have to ask a woman that question.

607
00:32:57,460 --> 00:33:00,340
There was good reason they called the first day of the week

608
00:33:00,440 --> 00:33:01,240
Blue Monday.

609
00:33:06,580 --> 00:33:11,580
Finally, the ever-evolving machine yielded in the 1890s

610
00:33:11,680 --> 00:33:13,980
to some form of rotary machine, which

611
00:33:14,080 --> 00:33:17,620
became the basis of almost all washing machines.

612
00:33:17,720 --> 00:33:23,060
This idea was refined during the 1920s and 30s by Maytag

613
00:33:23,160 --> 00:33:26,640
and became an agitator within a vertical drum.

614
00:33:26,740 --> 00:33:28,940
But the story does not end here.

615
00:33:32,880 --> 00:33:34,980
Early washing machines were created by engineers

616
00:33:34,981 --> 00:33:37,880
The washing machines were created by engineers.

617
00:33:37,980 --> 00:33:39,920
Now it was the designer's turn.

618
00:33:40,020 --> 00:33:44,560
It was time to turn the old Kelvinator into a work of art.

619
00:33:44,660 --> 00:33:48,760
Sears and Roebuck Company hired the esteemed Henry Dreyfus,

620
00:33:48,860 --> 00:33:51,700
one of the giants of industrial design.

621
00:33:51,800 --> 00:33:55,840
He was to be a creator of icons, the Princess Telephone,

622
00:33:55,940 --> 00:33:57,640
the West Clock Big Ben.

623
00:33:57,740 --> 00:34:03,200
But first, he was to create a beautiful washing machine.

624
00:34:03,201 --> 00:34:06,700
What Dreyfus did was to conceal the machine's working parts

625
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:09,440
inside a smooth, shapely shell.

626
00:34:09,540 --> 00:34:12,080
All was hidden but the nuts and bolts.

627
00:34:12,180 --> 00:34:15,080
It became the top-a-rater.

628
00:34:15,180 --> 00:34:18,140
But the washing machine was hardly the only appliance

629
00:34:18,240 --> 00:34:21,320
for cleaning deeply affected by design.

630
00:34:21,420 --> 00:34:24,380
In 20 years, the vacuum cleaner went from the streamlined

631
00:34:24,480 --> 00:34:28,020
looks of a locomotive to Sputnik sailing through space.

632
00:34:33,200 --> 00:34:34,700
It was a very good job.

633
00:34:37,700 --> 00:34:39,500
Early attempts at vacuum cleaning

634
00:34:39,600 --> 00:34:42,640
included this so-called portable machine.

635
00:34:42,740 --> 00:34:45,940
Requiring many hands, it was actually pushed around the house

636
00:34:46,040 --> 00:34:46,940
to do the job.

637
00:34:49,960 --> 00:34:51,620
But it took a nagging health problem

638
00:34:51,720 --> 00:34:55,180
to become the inspiration for a successful invention,

639
00:34:55,280 --> 00:34:56,520
this time in Canton, Ohio.

640
00:34:59,120 --> 00:35:02,560
In 1907, Murray Spangler, a janitor

641
00:35:02,561 --> 00:35:05,800
in a department store developed an irritating allergy

642
00:35:05,900 --> 00:35:08,260
from carpet dust.

643
00:35:08,360 --> 00:35:11,200
Working in his spare hours, he invented a vacuum cleaner

644
00:35:11,300 --> 00:35:15,200
with an old electric fan motor and a pillowcase.

645
00:35:15,300 --> 00:35:19,380
He rebuilt it and rebuilt it, experimenting constantly

646
00:35:19,480 --> 00:35:21,920
on the acres of carpet at the store,

647
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:25,040
and finally got a patent and started his electric suction

648
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:28,220
sweeper company.

649
00:35:28,320 --> 00:35:30,260
But critically short on funds, Spangler

650
00:35:30,261 --> 00:35:33,100
ended up showing one of his machines to a cousin,

651
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:36,440
a Mrs. William H. Hoover, whose husband had a leather

652
00:35:36,540 --> 00:35:38,200
and saddlery business.

653
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:40,500
Well, when Mr. Hoover saw the machine,

654
00:35:40,600 --> 00:35:42,240
he liked what he saw.

655
00:35:42,340 --> 00:35:46,100
And what he saw was a new future.

656
00:35:46,200 --> 00:35:50,220
William Hoover had great luck in acquiring this new cleaner.

657
00:35:50,320 --> 00:35:52,820
But he also had great foresight in realizing

658
00:35:52,920 --> 00:35:55,280
that door-to-door selling would be the answer

659
00:35:55,380 --> 00:35:56,580
to its merchandising.

660
00:36:00,260 --> 00:36:04,140
Like Hoover, other vacuum cleaner manufacturers soon realized

661
00:36:04,240 --> 00:36:07,060
their product could only be sold successfully

662
00:36:07,160 --> 00:36:09,540
by home demonstration.

663
00:36:11,440 --> 00:36:13,400
And what they carried in their cases

664
00:36:13,500 --> 00:36:16,840
soon became showpieces of design.

665
00:36:16,940 --> 00:36:20,480
Some see an automobile grill on the front of the 1930s Hoover

666
00:36:20,580 --> 00:36:22,620
Upright, and others see a train.

667
00:36:22,720 --> 00:36:25,280
With the front headlight on that vacuum cleaner,

668
00:36:25,380 --> 00:36:29,080
I've had that out at night, and it is eerie.

669
00:36:29,081 --> 00:36:32,420
That is a train coming down the center track.

670
00:36:32,520 --> 00:36:34,920
And I tell you, mom was using that,

671
00:36:35,020 --> 00:36:37,320
but there was a lot of happy kids who

672
00:36:37,420 --> 00:36:40,360
were playing with their Lionel trains in the 30s.

673
00:36:40,460 --> 00:36:43,700
But mom's vacuum cleaner was the train to own.

674
00:36:43,800 --> 00:36:46,700
And in the 50s, rockets, missiles, and satellites

675
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:50,600
replaced planes and trains, as in this Westinghouse model.

676
00:36:50,700 --> 00:36:53,400
That particular one, to me, reminds

677
00:36:53,500 --> 00:36:59,080
one of early Cape Canaveral, Saturn and Atlas rockets.

678
00:36:59,081 --> 00:37:00,920
You have indeed a lunar sled.

679
00:37:01,020 --> 00:37:02,880
I mean, you have like the vacuum in the front,

680
00:37:02,980 --> 00:37:05,480
but you have the thrust coming out the back.

681
00:37:05,580 --> 00:37:08,220
You needed some other technology, and mom would have

682
00:37:08,320 --> 00:37:10,420
had an airborne vacuum cleaner.

683
00:37:10,520 --> 00:37:12,620
But whether it was made with a pillowcase

684
00:37:12,720 --> 00:37:15,320
or made to look like it could land on the moon,

685
00:37:15,420 --> 00:37:17,540
lightening the load of dirt and dust collecting

686
00:37:17,640 --> 00:37:20,440
was liberating for the American homemaker.

687
00:37:20,540 --> 00:37:22,680
And the time and toil saved could now

688
00:37:22,780 --> 00:37:27,380
be used on other ingenious inventions of domestic utility,

689
00:37:27,381 --> 00:37:32,820
like the icon of the breakfast table, the toaster.

690
00:37:32,920 --> 00:37:36,820
Steam irons originally had just one to three holes.

691
00:37:36,920 --> 00:37:40,220
But by 1950, fierce competition made additional holes

692
00:37:40,320 --> 00:37:42,120
a major selling point.

693
00:37:42,220 --> 00:37:47,640
In 1971, a model by Presto won the so-called iron holy war

694
00:37:47,740 --> 00:37:49,040
with 70 holes.

695
00:37:53,440 --> 00:37:56,880
The kitchen of tomorrow is calling me.

696
00:37:56,881 --> 00:37:59,820
My cake is ready.

697
00:37:59,920 --> 00:38:03,080
Perhaps the kitchen reflects America's high standard

698
00:38:03,180 --> 00:38:06,960
of living more than any other room in the house.

699
00:38:07,060 --> 00:38:10,960
It is a wonderland of electrical appliances, gadgets,

700
00:38:11,060 --> 00:38:13,500
and space-age housewares.

701
00:38:13,600 --> 00:38:16,560
Yet just 90 years ago, most manufacturers

702
00:38:16,660 --> 00:38:19,600
thought that the best place for a toaster to be sold

703
00:38:19,700 --> 00:38:23,740
was not at the general store, but at an electrical generating

704
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:24,740
station.

705
00:38:26,880 --> 00:38:30,120
The toaster, radiant, polished silver icon

706
00:38:30,220 --> 00:38:33,020
of the breakfast table, so pure in design

707
00:38:33,120 --> 00:38:35,860
that some believe it belongs on a pedestal instead

708
00:38:35,960 --> 00:38:37,560
of the kitchen counter.

709
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:40,300
I bought those pieces for their absolute beauty,

710
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:44,360
their sculptural quality, their organic design, the color

711
00:38:44,460 --> 00:38:48,400
palette, and I see them as art.

712
00:38:48,500 --> 00:38:50,500
Toasters as art?

713
00:38:50,600 --> 00:38:54,000
Perhaps, but certainly not at their beginning.

714
00:38:54,001 --> 00:38:57,840
In the late 1800s, tin, wire, and porcelain stovetop

715
00:38:57,940 --> 00:38:59,740
toasters were introduced.

716
00:38:59,840 --> 00:39:02,800
Called an indispensable article, they

717
00:39:02,900 --> 00:39:08,540
boasted the ability to toast four slices in two minutes.

718
00:39:08,640 --> 00:39:12,360
But it was not until 1910 when these conveniences were finally

719
00:39:12,460 --> 00:39:15,660
plugged in that the modern toaster had arrived.

720
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:29,840
The next 20 years saw strange and wondrous concepts

721
00:39:29,940 --> 00:39:31,800
for the toaster.

722
00:39:31,900 --> 00:39:35,940
In fact, toasters weren't just toasters.

723
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:38,940
The idea of an appliance built for just one function

724
00:39:39,040 --> 00:39:41,380
was still a bit strange.

725
00:39:41,480 --> 00:39:44,620
And starting around 1915 through the 1920s,

726
00:39:44,720 --> 00:39:48,320
toasters became combinations.

727
00:39:48,420 --> 00:39:54,000
Advertising read, it toasts, it cooks, it brews.

728
00:39:54,001 --> 00:39:56,160
Sometimes there was a percolator built into it.

729
00:39:56,260 --> 00:39:59,000
Occasionally, you could make bacon and eggs on the same unit.

730
00:39:59,100 --> 00:40:00,540
It was called a table oven.

731
00:40:00,640 --> 00:40:02,440
Even that mixer is a big work saver.

732
00:40:02,540 --> 00:40:03,440
Oh, god.

733
00:40:03,540 --> 00:40:07,540
What kind of cream.

734
00:40:07,640 --> 00:40:11,860
Imagine having to do that the hard way, by hand.

735
00:40:11,960 --> 00:40:14,060
The evolution of the household mixer

736
00:40:14,160 --> 00:40:16,920
didn't stir up as much attention as the toaster,

737
00:40:17,020 --> 00:40:18,760
but it was certainly no less important.

738
00:40:18,761 --> 00:40:24,500
Its first name was the beater, and it was a modern servant

739
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:27,140
that became wildly popular with the new stoves

740
00:40:27,240 --> 00:40:29,340
of the 19th century.

741
00:40:29,440 --> 00:40:30,880
Once you could regulate temperatures,

742
00:40:30,980 --> 00:40:34,080
all kinds of new types of foods became available

743
00:40:34,180 --> 00:40:36,240
as ingredients for baking.

744
00:40:36,340 --> 00:40:40,420
And that meant more work for mother.

745
00:40:40,520 --> 00:40:43,920
Once or twice a year, a woman would make a cake.

746
00:40:44,020 --> 00:40:45,880
When the egg beater came on the market,

747
00:40:45,980 --> 00:40:47,180
it made it much easier.

748
00:40:47,181 --> 00:40:50,620
So people began eating cake much more often.

749
00:40:50,720 --> 00:40:53,980
So in a sense, if you just isolate cake making,

750
00:40:54,080 --> 00:40:56,220
women were doing more work with the egg beater

751
00:40:56,320 --> 00:40:58,520
because they were making more cakes.

752
00:40:58,620 --> 00:41:02,420
But it was the invention of the so easy universal home motor

753
00:41:02,520 --> 00:41:09,240
by Chester Beach in 1910 that turned the beater into a mixer.

754
00:41:09,340 --> 00:41:11,540
Then KitchenAid entered the market.

755
00:41:11,640 --> 00:41:15,580
In 1919, four mixers a day rolled off their Ohio assembly

756
00:41:15,581 --> 00:41:20,580
their Ohio assembly line, they sold for $189.50

757
00:41:20,680 --> 00:41:24,580
and were sold door to door by women.

758
00:41:24,680 --> 00:41:27,600
It was a very heavy and expensive machine in 1918

759
00:41:27,700 --> 00:41:31,260
when women sales force were carrying the 65 pound machine

760
00:41:31,360 --> 00:41:33,840
from door to door and demonstrating it.

761
00:41:33,940 --> 00:41:35,860
But it was the Sunbeam Mix Master

762
00:41:35,960 --> 00:41:39,580
introduced in 1930 that really became the fixture

763
00:41:39,680 --> 00:41:41,980
on kitchen counters across America.

764
00:41:41,981 --> 00:41:45,380
Its first year, it sold 60,000 mixers.

765
00:41:45,480 --> 00:41:49,220
By 1936, 300,000 a year.

766
00:41:49,320 --> 00:41:54,920
As its ad said, it mixes every atom thoroughly.

767
00:41:55,020 --> 00:41:57,120
In 1930, when it was first introduced,

768
00:41:57,220 --> 00:42:01,640
it really was a miracle of household operation.

769
00:42:01,740 --> 00:42:04,040
It did everything from polishing silver.

770
00:42:04,140 --> 00:42:06,280
You could buy attachments for juicing oranges,

771
00:42:06,380 --> 00:42:09,640
and shelling peas, and making sausages, and everything else.

772
00:42:11,980 --> 00:42:15,480
The history of household machines

773
00:42:15,580 --> 00:42:18,780
has been a chronicle of how our common daily lives

774
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:20,420
have changed.

775
00:42:20,520 --> 00:42:23,420
But what of the promise of tomorrow, the kitchen

776
00:42:23,520 --> 00:42:24,920
of the future?

777
00:42:25,020 --> 00:42:26,600
What new wonders will make our lives at home

778
00:42:26,700 --> 00:42:29,000
even more of a pleasure?

779
00:42:29,100 --> 00:42:32,040
My favorite answer to what the dream kitchen of tomorrow

780
00:42:32,140 --> 00:42:36,400
should be was actually something that was said by a woman in 1943

781
00:42:36,500 --> 00:42:39,340
when she answered the question, the question

782
00:42:39,341 --> 00:42:43,240
by a woman in 1943 when she answered the McCall's Home

783
00:42:43,241 --> 00:42:46,340
of Tomorrow survey, which asked what you wanted your dream

784
00:42:46,341 --> 00:42:47,640
kitchen to be.

785
00:42:47,641 --> 00:42:54,240
And she said, my dream kitchen of tomorrow is no kitchen at all.

786
00:42:54,241 --> 00:42:57,140
My dream kitchen is one in which I sit in the dining room

787
00:42:57,141 --> 00:43:02,140
and the meals emerge as a complete surprise to me.

788
00:43:02,141 --> 00:43:08,440
So I think almost 50 years later that that remains for many

789
00:43:08,441 --> 00:43:12,140
their ultimate dream kitchen is, in fact,

790
00:43:12,240 --> 00:43:14,240
to be free of the kitchen.

791
00:43:14,340 --> 00:43:15,680
Think of it.

792
00:43:15,780 --> 00:43:18,820
No more recipes, no more meals to plan,

793
00:43:18,920 --> 00:43:24,460
nothing to cook, nothing to prepare, no worries, not a care.

794
00:43:24,560 --> 00:43:26,760
We are free at last to concern ourselves

795
00:43:26,860 --> 00:43:28,920
with the world outside the kitchen

796
00:43:29,020 --> 00:43:31,600
and things of greater consequence.

797
00:43:31,700 --> 00:43:34,740
At last, we can move ourselves to the living room

798
00:43:34,840 --> 00:43:38,140
and spend some quality time with an even more

799
00:43:38,141 --> 00:43:41,140
important household invention, the television.

